WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:06.460
Black Panther gave us a spectacular big-screen
introduction to Wakanda with a politically-charged

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:10.620
and emotional thriller that has important
implications for the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:14.750
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here
and today I'm discussing the thrilling ending

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:18.380
to Black Panther and what it all means for
future Marvel movies.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:22.640
In case you don't know already, I do regular
videos about superhero movies, and I'm currently

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.980
also running a Black Panther giveaway.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:29.070
For a chance to win, be sure to subscribe
and leave a comment below about the movie.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:32.520
Obviously spoilers ahead, so take care if
you haven't seen Black Panther yet.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:37.520
There are a number of themes woven into Black
Panther that get some resolution by the end

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.730
of the movie, but let's start with the very
final scene before the credits, which is when

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:46.000
T'Challa and Shuri visit the basketball court
and apartment block in Oakland where the movie's

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.920
opening flashback scene was set.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.220
There's an interesting symmetry between the
beginning and the end of the film which demonstrates

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:56.400
T'Challa has made a concrete decision to lead
a positive change in the world and correct

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:02.010
the wrongs committed by his father T'Chaka
and Zuri, aka James, back in 1992, when they

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:05.990
confronted T'Chaka's brother about helping
Klaue steal vibranium.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:08.580
It was in Oakland where T'Chaka killed his
brother N'Jobu.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:13.229
Though he did this to save Zuri, his actions
sowed the seeds of anger and resentment in

00:01:13.229 --> 00:01:18.280
Prince N'Jobu's son Erik, aka Killmonger,
when they chose to abandon him rather than

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:19.479
take care of him.

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:24.579
Unlike his father, T'Challa is determined
to stop operating in secret and he openly

00:01:24.579 --> 00:01:27.579
lands his Royal Talon Fighter on the basketball
court.

00:01:27.579 --> 00:01:31.950
Both in this scene and the subsequent mid-credits
scene, T'Challa proves that he's ready to

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:34.159
reveal the truth about Wakanda.

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:38.909
He also wants Wakanda to have a positive impact
on the world by converting the apartment block,

00:01:38.909 --> 00:01:42.530
which was the location of the tragedy in the
flashback scene, as well as the buildings

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:47.420
either side of it, into a Wakandan outreach
centre, and he appoints Nakia as head of social

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:51.009
outreach while Shuri will spear-head a science
exchange.

00:01:51.009 --> 00:01:55.079
Of course, it's just coincidence, but given
that Sterling K. Brown plays N'Jobu, this

00:01:55.079 --> 00:01:59.369
moment made me think of the TV drama This
Is Us and how Brown's character Randall buys

00:01:59.369 --> 00:02:03.240
the run-down building where his father had
lived so he could improve life for everyone

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:04.240
living there.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:09.480
That final Oakland scene also shows the culmination
of T'Challa's arc in the movie by proving

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:12.400
that he could be king and still be a good
man.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.790
If you're wondering what I mean by this, remember
when T'Challa visited his father on the ancestral

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:20.200
plane after ingesting the Heart-Shaped Herb
for the first time?

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:23.680
Well, T'Chaka told his son, "You're a good
man, with a good heart.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.080
And it's hard for a good man to be king."

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:30.230
T'Challa later learned what his father meant
by this when Zuri revealed how he'd helped

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:34.629
T'Chaka hide the truth about what happened
to N'Jobu and his son Erik.

00:02:34.629 --> 00:02:38.670
Finally, during their second meeting on the
ancestral plane, T'Chaka admitted to his son

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:42.019
that he'd chosen Wakanda and its people over
his nephew.

00:02:42.019 --> 00:02:46.799
And that lie about the events in Oakland,
that truth which T'Chaka chose to bury for

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:51.460
many years actually, as T'Challa puts it,
ended up creating a monster in the form of

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:52.930
Killmonger.

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:57.230
These moments of revelation in Black Panther
reminded me of Thor: Ragnarok – spoiler

00:02:57.230 --> 00:03:01.760
alert if you've not seen it – because we
discovered in that film that Odin had hidden

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:05.780
his daughter Hela and her role in violently
conquering the Nine Realms.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:09.840
Each of these stories involves concealing
both the existence of relatives – T'Challa's

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:14.760
cousin and Thor's sister – and the violence
sometimes used to maintain seemingly benevolent

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:15.760
rule.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:19.439
And of course, in both stories, it's the sons
who are left dealing with the damage caused

00:03:19.439 --> 00:03:21.590
by their fathers' actions.

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:26.170
T'Challa is appalled by what T'Chaka did and
takes Nakia's advice not to let his father's

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:27.770
mistakes define him.

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:32.140
As she said, "you get to decide what kind
of king you are going to be."

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:35.780
And that's exactly what T'Challa does when
he refuses to stay with his father in the

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:38.500
ancestral plane the second time they meet
there.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:43.120
Instead of joining his ancestors, T'Challa
chooses to return to Wakanda and prove he

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:48.540
can be a good man and a good king by affecting
real change in the world, sharing his nation's

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:51.849
resources and technology for the betterment
of others.

00:03:51.849 --> 00:03:55.969
By the end of the film, T'Challa's eyes have
been opened by both Nakia, who told him she

00:03:55.969 --> 00:04:00.730
had "seen too many in need to turn a blind
eye" any longer, and also by the events that

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:03.849
led Erik on the path to become Killmonger.

00:04:03.849 --> 00:04:07.510
Killmonger's death is another crucial moment
in Black Panther.

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:11.629
In keeping with his beliefs, he preferred
to die than be imprisoned, and his death in

00:04:11.629 --> 00:04:15.930
front of the Wakandan sunset was an emotional
link back to the words he shared with his

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:19.650
father about Wakandan sunsets being the most
beautiful in the world.

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:24.360
In addition to the symmetry between the film's
opening and closing scenes in Oakland, there's

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:28.570
also a certain symmetry around the fates of
Killmonger and his father N'Jobu, each of

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:32.699
whom died in complicated circumstances at
the hands of a relative.

00:04:32.699 --> 00:04:37.200
T'Chaka had wanted his brother to face Wakanda's
council for his crimes, but ended up killing

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:38.949
him to save Zuri.

00:04:38.949 --> 00:04:42.970
While T'Challa was defending himself from
Killmonger and Wakanda from his rule, but

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:46.539
ultimately offered his cousin the healing
power of Wakandan technology.

00:04:46.539 --> 00:04:51.509
As the movie's main antagonist, Killmonger's
world view was very much in opposition to

00:04:51.509 --> 00:04:56.530
Black Panther's outlook, but then the two
men had very different upbringings.

00:04:56.530 --> 00:05:00.680
T'Challa was raised in Wakanda, which due
to the vibranium meteorite, was able to isolate

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:04.710
itself from the world and was therefore never
subject to the colonialism experienced by

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:06.449
their African neighbours.

00:05:06.449 --> 00:05:10.800
On the other hand, in many ways, Killmonger
represents the African experience as a result

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.610
of colonialism, slavery and racism.

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:17.680
Erik was brought up in Oakland, which is both
the birthplace of the film's director Ryan

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:23.949
Coogler and the city where, in 1966, the real-life
Black Panther Party began with a focus on

00:05:23.949 --> 00:05:27.380
combating police brutality against the black
community.

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:32.690
By the way, 1966 was also the year Black Panther
first appeared in Marvel comics.

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:37.789
In Coogler's film, as we learn from Zuri,
Killmonger's father N'Jobu experienced discrimination

00:05:37.789 --> 00:05:42.110
in Oakland and that led him to act against
Wakandan policy by smuggling out vibranium

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:44.110
in an effort to fight back.

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:47.700
When Killmonger takes over as ruler of Wakanda,
he declares that the sun will "never set on

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:53.340
the Wakandan empire", echoing the phrase used
to describe the former Spanish and British

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:58.220
Empires, which were so large it was said always
to be daytime somewhere.

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:02.410
Killmonger further overturns Wakandan traditions
when he orders the burning of the heart-shaped

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:07.379
herb, and director Ryan Coogler reflects Killmonger's
revolution and the fact he is turning how

00:06:07.379 --> 00:06:12.150
Wakanda operates completely on its head by
having the camera revolve 180 degrees from

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:16.100
an upside-down position as the new king enters
the throne room.

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:20.300
Michael B. Jordan has said of Killmonger that,
"He is always ten steps ahead.

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:21.319
He's very patient.

00:06:21.319 --> 00:06:25.750
That's a very dangerous attribute to have
as a villain because he's going to sit and

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:29.849
wait, and he's going to plan and calculate
every move."

00:06:29.849 --> 00:06:33.569
Killmonger gained both tactical and military
skills on combat missions in countries such

00:06:33.569 --> 00:06:37.789
as Afghanistan and Iraq, and he appears to
kill his girlfriend and then also Klaue, who

00:06:37.789 --> 00:06:42.410
he'd been collaborating with, seemingly without
hesitation when he deemed it necessary to

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:44.620
getting into Wakanda.

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:49.009
Killmonger is incredibly effective and ruthless
in carrying out his plan to take over the

00:06:49.009 --> 00:06:50.009
nation.

00:06:50.009 --> 00:06:53.190
Yet, despite his extremes, you can empathise
with his grievances.

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:55.780
In short, he's an incredible antagonist.

00:06:55.780 --> 00:07:00.100
And although, ultimately, Killmonger fails
to complete his plans, the fact that T'Challa

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:04.900
was deeply affected by the experiences of
N'Jobu and Killmonger and the actions of T'Chaka

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:09.080
means that other kids from Oakland can now
believe in Wakanda and its incredible technological

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:12.430
advances without thinking it's just a fairy
tale.

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:17.430
It really is good to see Marvel developing
their villains better in recent movies and,

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:21.991
Michael B. Jordan is such a fantastic actor
who brought so much to this film that it's

00:07:21.991 --> 00:07:26.599
a real shame to think he won't be returning,
but Killmonger did have a great arc.

00:07:26.599 --> 00:07:31.289
The other great secondary villain in the movie
was Ulysses Klaue, played in a brilliantly

00:07:31.289 --> 00:07:36.460
unhinged and humorous way by Andy Serkis,
and again, it's a pity his character is dead.

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:41.039
Of course, these are comic book movies, so
technically any character can still come back

00:07:41.039 --> 00:07:42.039
from the dead.

00:07:42.039 --> 00:07:45.909
In fact, there is one Marvel villain I do
think could make a stunning return in the

00:07:45.909 --> 00:07:51.750
MCU for a very good reason, and that's a certain
green goddess and you can check out my theory

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:54.669
on why I think we'll see her again right here.

00:07:54.669 --> 00:07:59.289
An important recommendation that T'Challa
followed from his father was to surround himself

00:07:59.289 --> 00:08:03.569
with good people and it was his little sister
Shuri who played a huge role in helping him

00:08:03.569 --> 00:08:05.169
take back the throne.

00:08:05.169 --> 00:08:10.379
She and Nakia are extremely loyal to T'Challa
though both of them have a rebellious streak

00:08:10.379 --> 00:08:14.069
and they encourage the young king to push
beyond the status quo.

00:08:14.069 --> 00:08:19.120
Shuri herself is constantly pushing herself
to another level, actively developing and

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:23.660
improving her tech; indeed, as she told her
brother, "just because it works, doesn't mean

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:25.530
it can't be improved."

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:30.110
In fact, that's something I think T'Challa
takes on board in opening up Wakanda.

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:33.919
And it was great to see Shuri so excited in
the final scene when her brother announced

00:08:33.919 --> 00:08:37.329
she was going to lead the Wakandan technology
exchange with the rest of the world.

00:08:37.329 --> 00:08:41.919
I can't wait to see what tech she gets to
develop in Avengers: Infinity War and I'd

00:08:41.919 --> 00:08:46.790
love to see her meet Tony Stark at some point
as I think their banter and talk about her

00:08:46.790 --> 00:08:49.060
amazing inventions could be fantastic.

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:53.579
There's also the possibility she could don
a Black Panther suit of her own in the future

00:08:53.579 --> 00:08:57.740
and there's a hint at that via the toothed
neckpiece she wears in some scenes in the

00:08:57.740 --> 00:08:58.740
film.

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:02.079
By the way, I think it's interesting that
the new Wakandan centre will be established

00:09:02.079 --> 00:09:06.510
in Oakland because the next Marvel movie after
Avengers: Infinity War is Ant-Man And The

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:12.000
Wasp, which is set in San Francisco and, geographically,
Oakland is just across the bay.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:16.820
In fact, the Oakland Bay Bridge made an appearance
in the first Ant-Man And The Wasp trailer,

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:20.440
and I wonder whether there's a chance we could
see Black Panther or some Wakandan characters

00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:24.070
cross over into that movie later this year?

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:27.910
Another thing T'Challa achieves by the end
of the film is a sort of repairing of relations

00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:33.140
with the Jabari tribe, whose leader M'Baku
does not take kindly to his request that they

00:09:33.140 --> 00:09:38.200
join him against Killmonger as previous kings
have not bothered to visit them in centuries.

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:42.880
T'Challa spared M'Baku's life during their
ritual combat for the throne, but M'Baku already

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:47.630
repaid that debt when he helped save T'Challa
after Killmonger flung him off the side of

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:48.630
a mountain.

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:52.920
Still, in the end, M'Baku and his people do
rally to T'Challa's cause of saving Wakanda

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:57.620
from Killmonger, because they realise, as
Black Panther explained, that this affects

00:09:57.620 --> 00:09:58.870
everyone.

00:09:58.870 --> 00:10:03.100
And in doing this M'Baku and his tribe have
a huge impact on the final battle.

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:08.889
And talking of the final battle, the Dora
Milaje also play a key role with Okoye literally

00:10:08.889 --> 00:10:13.420
bringing a rhino to a standstill by fearlessly
confronting her love W'Kabi as soon as she

00:10:13.420 --> 00:10:16.270
realises that Killmonger is not the rightful
king.

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:21.779
She's an incredible fighter, as are her Dora
Milaje sisters, and I can't wait to see more

00:10:21.779 --> 00:10:22.800
of them on screen.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:28.190
Now, I spoke about how important the final
scene is in showing how T'Challa is transforming

00:10:28.190 --> 00:10:31.380
and opening up Wakandan society to the wider
world.

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:36.050
And this could have a big effect on the rest
of the MCU which we see the beginnings of

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:39.149
when T'Challa addresses the United Nations
in the first end-credits scene.

00:10:39.149 --> 00:10:43.370
And I've actually got a separate video with
a full breakdown of that scene plus the final

00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:47.790
stinger scene with Bucky and what it all means
for Avengers: Infinity War, and you can watch

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:52.399
that by tapping right up here, or clicking
the link in the video description below.

